INT. COIN LAUNDRY - NIGHT

Dryers tumble. Plastic chairs. A vending machine hums.

Six regulars huddle around a folding table. They're holding a wake for the shop's owner. He's laid out in a framed photo between the detergent boxes.

FRANKIE DOLAN lifts a paper cup. MEI-LING WONG nods. OBI NWANKWO wipes his eyes. SABINE ARCAND folds a towel without looking. RUFUS BELL stares at the floor. The youngest, TAMSIN PRICE, taps her phone.

FRANKIE
To Gus.

MEI-LING
To Gus.

OBI
Sixty years of quarters.

RUFUS
He never gave me change once.

SABINE
He gave me change twice.

TAMSIN
He gave me a free sock.

They all look at her.

OBI
A sock?

TAMSIN
One. He said the other would turn up.

FRANKIE
It always does.

MEI-LING
Never.

They laugh, quietly, then stop. The dryers keep turning.

FRANKIE
I'll miss him.

^RUFUS
I'll miss him.

They glance at one another, startled by the echo. She puts her phone away. He swallows. The vending machine thuds out a bag of chips.

FADE OUT.
